//! The Basic module: the bottom of the Jairs standard library.
//!
//! This is written in Jairs, not in the compiler (PLAN.md decision #5). That is
//! what forces `#foreign` into the first slice: the bottom of a standard library
//! is a syscall, and there is no way to express it otherwise.
//!
//! Everything here must be writable in the Jairs-0 subset. That constraint is
//! real and it bites -- see the note on integer printing at the end of this file.

libc :: #system_library "c";

/// POSIX write(2): writes `count` bytes from `buf` to the file descriptor `fd`.
///
/// Jairs strings are `{data: *u8, count: s64}` and are NOT NUL-terminated (ADR-0004),
/// which is exactly the shape `write` wants: a pointer and a length. No conversion, no
/// temporary storage, no `to_c_string`.
write :: (fd: s64, buf: *u8, count: s64) -> s64 #foreign libc "write";

/// Terminates the process with `status`.
///
/// Declared here so programs have a way out that does not depend on `main` returning,
/// which the slice does not model yet.
exit :: (status: s64) #foreign libc "exit";

/// Allocates `size` bytes and returns a pointer to them, or `null` on failure (ADR-0060 §2).
///
/// The honest bottom of a standard library until wave W7 replaces it: libc's own `malloc`. A
/// `#foreign` pointer return is an ordinary register scalar (ADR-0051), so this needs no new
/// machinery. It cannot be called at *compile time* — ADR-0006 gates comptime FFI, and a host
/// pointer read through the VM's own address space would be a plausible wrong value.
malloc :: (size: s64) -> *u8 #foreign libc "malloc";

/// Releases memory obtained from [`malloc`]. Passing `null` is defined (a no-op), which is why a
/// program can `free` unconditionally.
free :: (p: *u8) #foreign libc "free";

/// The size of the temporary-storage arena, in bytes (ADR-0065 §2).
///
/// Fixed rather than growable: a growable arena would either move on `realloc` — invalidating every
/// pointer `talloc` already returned — or need a block list, which is more than the slice wants. 64
/// KiB is enough for the scratch uses here; a later wave can make it a context field.
TEMP_REGION_SIZE :: 65536;

/// Allocates `n` bytes from the per-context temporary-storage arena (ADR-0065).
///
/// The bytes stay valid until the next [`reset_temporary_storage`]; there is no per-piece free. This
/// is the allocator for scratch space a computation throws away wholesale — building a string to
/// print, say — where freeing each piece would be noise.
///
/// The arena is `context.temp_data`, a region lazily `malloc`'d on first use (it is null in a fresh
/// context, ADR-0057 §5), and `context.temp_mark` is the bump cursor — a *byte count*, so the next
/// allocation is at `temp_data + temp_mark`. Returns `null` on a failed `malloc` or when the region is
/// full, matching [`malloc`]'s convention (ADR-0065 §3): running out of scratch is a resource
/// condition a caller may handle, not a bug that should trap.
///
/// It reads the context, so it travels with the call: a callee's `talloc` uses its caller's arena.
talloc :: (n: s64) -> *u8 {
    if context.temp_data == null {
        context.temp_data = malloc(TEMP_REGION_SIZE);
        if context.temp_data == null {
            return null;
        }
    }
    if context.temp_mark + n > TEMP_REGION_SIZE {
        return null;
    }
    // `*u8 + s64` is byte arithmetic (element-scaled on a `*u8`, which is what a byte arena wants,
    // ADR-0064 §2), so this is the address `temp_mark` bytes into the region.
    p := context.temp_data + context.temp_mark;
    context.temp_mark = context.temp_mark + n;
    return p;
}

/// Rewinds the temporary-storage cursor, freeing everything [`talloc`] handed out at once (ADR-0065).
///
/// It does **not** release the region — the `malloc`'d 64 KiB stays for the program's life, because
/// reuse is the point of an arena. It rewinds `context.temp_mark` to 0, so the next `talloc` hands out
/// from the start again. A pointer from before the reset is dangling afterwards, exactly as it would
/// be after a `free` — the arena's contract is "valid until the next reset".
reset_temporary_storage :: () {
    context.temp_mark = 0;
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Runtime type information (ADR-0075)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `type_info(T)` returns a `*Type_Info` describing `T`. The struct is declared **here, in Jairs**,
// rather than inside the compiler, and that is a deliberate decision with a cost.
//
// The reason is that a `Type_Info` must be *spellable*: a program that reflects has to be able to write
// `info: *Type_Info` and read `info.name`. No compiler-declared type is spellable — `t: Type;` and
// `c: Context;` both report E0212, because `Context`'s fields are a Rust `const` and a compiler-declared
// type has no `DeclId` for name resolution to find (ADR-0057 §1). Declaring the struct in Jairs makes it
// spellable for free: it is then an ordinary nominal declaration, and field access, layout, pointers and
// `using` all apply with no new case in the compiler.
//
// The cost is that the compiler now depends on a declaration it does not own. That dependency is
// **validated** rather than trusted: the compiler looks `Type_Info` up by name and checks its field
// names, types and order, refusing with E0265 if they do not match what it expects. Editing this struct
// therefore produces a diagnostic naming the mismatch, not a silent read of whatever is at offset 8 —
// which matters because a wrong offset is this project's named failure mode and a refusal is not.
//
// Keep the fields, their types and their order in step with `jr-sema`'s `TYPE_INFO_FIELDS`.

/// What kind of type a [`Type_Info`] describes.
///
/// An enum rather than an integer so that a `switch` over it is exhaustiveness-checked (ADR-0067),
/// which is the whole reason to have a tag at all. The members are the shapes the language has;
/// `#run`-only shapes such as the type of a type never reach here, because `type_info` refuses a type
/// with no runtime layout (E0266).
Type_Info_Kind :: enum {
    VOID;
    BOOL;
    INTEGER;
    FLOAT;
    STRING;
    POINTER;
    ARRAY;
    VIEW;
    STRUCT;
    UNION;
    VARIANT;
    ENUM;
    PROCEDURE;
}

/// What the compiler knows about a type at run time (ADR-0075 §3, ADR-0077).
///
/// Five fields since ADR-0077 added `id`; `name` is the one that made this wave need ADR-0075 §1 first: a compile-time
/// aggregate holding a string used to be refused, so a `Type_Info` could not carry a name.
///
/// Deliberately **without per-kind detail** — a struct's field list, an array's element type, a
/// procedure's signature. Each of those is a variable-length member, so it wants a view or a
/// pointer-and-count, and that raises a memory-ownership question (static data the back end emits,
/// versus a comptime-built table) which is its own decision. `size` and `alignment` are enough to be
/// useful — they are what `size_of` would give, which this language does not otherwise have — and the
/// shape extends by adding fields, which does not break a reader that names only these four.
Type_Info :: struct {
    /// The type's canonical identity — its pool id, widened to `s64` (ADR-0077).
    ///
    /// An opaque token, not a number to do arithmetic on: two `type_info(T)` calls carry the same `id`
    /// while two distinct types never do, which is what lets `any_as` check a value's type without
    /// comparing pointers (two `type_info(T)` calls live in different slots, so their addresses differ).
    id: s64;
    /// Which shape this type is.
    kind: Type_Info_Kind;
    /// The type's source name, or a builtin's spelling (`"s64"`).
    name: string;
    /// The type's runtime size in bytes.
    size: s64;
    /// The type's runtime alignment in bytes.
    alignment: s64;
}

/// A value whose type is carried alongside it (ADR-0076 §3).
///
/// `Any` is how a procedure takes an argument whose type it does not know: the `type` says what the
/// value is and `data` points at it. `any_of(*x)` builds one and `any_as(a, T)` reads it back, trapping
/// if the type does not match — the check is the whole point of carrying the type, and a read that
/// ignored it would make this a `void*` with extra steps.
///
/// Declared here rather than inside the compiler for the reason [`Type_Info`] is: a program has to be
/// able to *name* it — `takes :: (a: Any)` is the entire use case — and no compiler-declared type is
/// spellable. The compiler validates these fields on lookup, so editing them is a diagnostic rather
/// than a wrong read.
///
/// **`data` is a `*u8` and nothing is reinterpreted.** A pointer's layout does not depend on what it
/// points at, so erasing `*Point` to `*u8` loses no bits and emits no code — it is a statement in the
/// type system only. That is why the conversion is allowed *here* and a general pointer cast is not
/// (ADR-0076 §1): a general one would make a wrong pointee type a silent wrong read.
Any :: struct {
    /// What `data` points at.
    type: *Type_Info;
    /// The value itself, erased. Read it back with `any_as` rather than by casting.
    data: *u8;
}

/// The standard output file descriptor.
STDOUT :: 1;
/// The standard error file descriptor.
STDERR :: 2;

/// Writes a string to standard output.
print :: (s: string) {
    write(STDOUT, s.data, s.count);
}

/// Writes a string to standard error.
print_error :: (s: string) {
    write(STDERR, s.data, s.count);
}

/// Writes a string followed by a newline.
print_line :: (s: string) {
    print(s);
    print("\n");
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Module-private helpers (ADR-0054 §1)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything from here down is hidden from importers until the `#scope_export` below. These two are
// implementation detail: `put_byte` writes one byte through the address of a local, and
// `print_digits` is the recursion `print_int` drives. An importer needs neither — and hiding them
// means giving `print_digits` a buffer later cannot break a caller, because there are none outside
// this file.
//
// This is the dogfooding ADR-0054's consequences call for: if `#scope_module` is right, the standard
// library's own internals should be the first thing to use it.
#scope_module

/// Writes one byte to standard output.
///
/// The address of a local is what makes this expressible: `write` wants a `*u8` and a
/// count, and a one-byte local supplies both (ADR-0011's prefix `*`). There is no buffer
/// because `[N]u8` arrives with arrays; see `print_int`.
put_byte :: (b: u8) {
    p := *b;
    write(STDOUT, p, 1);
}

/// Writes the decimal digits of a non-negative number.
///
/// Recursive, and deliberately so: `print_int` has no buffer to format into, because
/// `[N]u8` arrives with arrays in a later wave. Recursion emits the digits in the right
/// order without one -- divide first, print the high digits, then this digit on the way
/// back out. It costs one frame per digit, which for a 64-bit number is at most 20.
///
/// This is the shape `PLAN.md` §1.2 predicted: the wave that unblocks integer printing is
/// the wave that adds `cast`, and the buffer is still owed. When arrays land, this becomes
/// a loop over a `[20]u8` and the recursion goes away.
print_digits :: (n: s64) {
    if n >= 10 {
        print_digits(n / 10);
    }
    // `n % 10` is 0..9, so `+ 48` is '0'..'9' and the cast cannot lose information --
    // which is why it is a cast and not a checked conversion (ADR-0037 §2).
    put_byte(cast(u8, n % 10 + 48));
}

#scope_export

/// Writes a number in decimal.
///
/// Handles the sign here rather than in `print_digits`, so the recursion has one job. Note
/// what is *not* handled: the most negative `s64` cannot be negated -- `-(-9223372036854775808)`
/// overflows, and ADR-0002 makes that a trap rather than a wrap. So this traps on it, which
/// is honest; printing it needs either an unsigned path or `-%`, and both are later waves.
print_int :: (n: s64) {
    if n < 0 {
        print("-");
        print_digits(-n);
    } else {
        print_digits(n);
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Integer printing: what it took, and what it still owes
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This section used to say `print_int` was not implementable. It is now above, and the
// thing that unblocked it was `cast` (ADR-0037): turning a digit value into a byte for
// `write` is an `s64` to `u8` conversion and nothing else would do.
//
// Still owed, and visible in the code above:
//
//   * a fixed-size buffer `[N]u8` to format into. `print_digits` recurses instead, one
//     frame per digit, because emitting digits high-end-first needs either a buffer or a
//     stack and only one of those exists yet.
//   * the most negative `s64`. `print_int` negates a negative, and negating
//     -9223372036854775808 overflows, which ADR-0002 makes a trap. Printing it needs an
//     unsigned path or the wrapping `-%` operator.
//
// Neither is faked. A wrong digit would be worse than a missing feature.
